Public vs Private Subnets — AWS Networking Basics

As part of my AWS Cloud Architect learning, I explored a key VPC concept: Public and Private Subnets.
Understanding this helps build secure, well-designed cloud architectures.

⸻

🔹 Public Subnet

A subnet that has a route to the Internet Gateway (IGW).
Resources here can communicate with the internet (inbound + outbound), depending on security groups.

Best for:
	•	Web servers
	•	ALB / NLB
	•	Bastion hosts

⸻

🔹 Private Subnet

A subnet without direct internet access.
If outbound internet is required, it goes through a NAT Gateway in the public subnet.

Best for:
	•	Databases (RDS)
	•	Application servers
	•	Internal microservices

⸻

🔹 Why Both Are Needed

Using public + private subnets creates a layered architecture:
✔ More security
✔ Better isolation
✔ Reduced attack surface
✔ Aligns with AWS best practices

⸻

🔥 In Simple Words

Public = “Can talk to the internet.”
Private = “Protected inside the VPC.”
